WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:08.610
Where Koshland argued enzymes must move, David Phillips made one hold still long

2
00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:09.610
enough to photograph.

3
00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:16.050
In 1965 he solved the first atom by atom structure of an enzyme hen egg white

4
00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:19.850
lysozyme, the very structure you can open and rotate from the rail.

5
00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:25.950
In a three-day burst of reasoning he saw not just the fold but the mechanism: the

6
00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:31.230
enzyme's deep cleft grips the sugar chain of a bacterial cell wall, and the sugar

7
00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:35.870
can't fit without bending into a strained half-chair, wrenched far enough out of

8
00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:37.930
true that its bonds are primed to snap.

9
00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:41.290
Catalysis had become a problem in engineering.

10
00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:46.890
A wartime radar officer reading invisible signals bouncing off objects he never saw

11
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:52.410
he trusted that kind of leap, and pressed a pragmatism on his students: "Don't let

12
00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.370
the best be the enemy of the good."

13
00:00:55.133 --> 00:01:01.133
The skeletal model he built, 129 amino acids folded at 100 million times life size,

14
00:01:01.553 --> 00:01:05.013
became the picture of an enzyme in every textbook that followed.
